WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.500
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:05.760
CHILD: Alexa, turn
off the lights.

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:06.675
AMAZON ALEXA: OK.

00:00:06.675 --> 00:00:10.890
CHILD: [GIGGLING] Alexa,
turn on the lights.

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:13.080
AMAZON ALEXA: OK.

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:16.680
SARAH HANSEN: Today on Chalk
Radio, robots and humans become

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:18.845
collaborative partners.

00:00:18.845 --> 00:00:20.220
CYNTHIA BREAZEAL:
It's not about,

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:22.620
let's build machines
that look like people

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:23.580
and act like people.

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:26.850
It's, let's build machines
that dovetail with us in a way

00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:29.670
that we can bring
forth all of ourselves

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:31.440
to help us achieve
the goals that

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:33.750
are really important to us.

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:36.300
SARAH HANSEN: I'm your
host, Sarah Hansen.

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:40.290
This week, we're talking with
a researcher and educator who's

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:43.230
working to bridge the gap
between human well-being

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:45.420
and artificial intelligence.

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:48.300
From kindergarten to
college classrooms,

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:50.310
she's preparing
the next generation

00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:55.245
of informed technology users
and empathetic designers.

00:00:55.245 --> 00:00:56.870
CYNTHIA BREAZEAL:
I'm Cynthia Breazeal.

00:00:56.870 --> 00:01:00.860
I am a professor at
MIT at the Media Lab.

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:05.630
I am also director of a
new MIT-wide initiative

00:01:05.630 --> 00:01:09.380
called Responsible AI for Social
Empowerment and Education.

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:11.210
We call it RAISE for short.

00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:16.122
And I very recently am the
Dean for Digital Learning.

00:01:16.122 --> 00:01:17.580
SARAH HANSEN:
Cynthia's fascination

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:20.550
with artificial intelligence
began a long time

00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:24.298
ago in a galaxy far, far away.

00:01:24.298 --> 00:01:26.340
CYNTHIA BREAZEAL: When I
was 10, I saw Star Wars.

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:30.030
And that forever
shaped my, I guess,

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:32.937
vision of what a
robot could be for us.

00:01:32.937 --> 00:01:34.770
I mean, it wasn't just
a mindless automaton.

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:37.035
It was an entity that had--

00:01:37.035 --> 00:01:39.035
in the movie, they were
full-fledged characters.

00:01:39.035 --> 00:01:39.535
Right?

00:01:39.535 --> 00:01:40.440
They had friendships.

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:43.170
They were mission-driven
to save the universe.

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:44.490
I mean, all of that.

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:46.652
And, of course, they
had relationships,

00:01:46.652 --> 00:01:48.360
not just with the
robots but with people.

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:49.400
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:51.150
SARAH HANSEN: Cynthia
followed that vision

00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:54.960
of complex human-machine
relationships all the way

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:57.810
to a PhD program at MIT.

00:01:57.810 --> 00:02:04.493
There she designed Kismet, the
world's first social robot.

00:02:04.493 --> 00:02:06.910
CYNTHIA BREAZEAL: That kind
of started this whole question

00:02:06.910 --> 00:02:09.310
of socially and emotionally
intelligent interaction

00:02:09.310 --> 00:02:12.340
with machines, because we
are profoundly socially

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:13.780
and emotional creatures.

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:15.250
If we want to
design technologies

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:17.930
that can interact with us and
treat us, again, as people,

00:02:17.930 --> 00:02:20.650
we need to understand
that dimension.

00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:22.570
A lot of the early
work was pushing

00:02:22.570 --> 00:02:24.890
on I would say the
algorithmic side of that.

00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:28.240
We looked at a lot of models
of emotion, and people,

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:30.620
and animals, and what
is the function of it?

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:32.110
And how might you
implement aspects

00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:35.290
of that for intelligent
communication and interaction

00:02:35.290 --> 00:02:36.560
with machines?

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:38.440
And then we started
thinking about, OK,

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:39.732
so what's this really good for?

00:02:39.732 --> 00:02:42.080
So it's fascinating from
a scientific perspective.

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:44.260
It's fascinating from a
technical perspective.

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:46.900
But what does this
mean about how

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:51.640
we will interact with robots,
with intelligent machines

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:53.030
in our lives, in our future.

00:02:53.030 --> 00:02:54.640
And so then we started
thinking about,

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:59.140
so what are these big societal
challenges that we face where

00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:02.710
potentially an AI cut more
from this kind of cloth

00:03:02.710 --> 00:03:05.422
or this philosophy might
really make a difference?

00:03:05.422 --> 00:03:06.880
SARAH HANSEN: After
Kismet, Cynthia

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:10.623
began working on a new
social robot named Jibo.

00:03:10.623 --> 00:03:12.040
CYNTHIA BREAZEAL:
Jibo is actually

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:14.440
these concentric
rotating circles

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:16.060
that are kind of off-axis.

00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:18.910
And with that, he
can kind of create

00:03:18.910 --> 00:03:20.810
what's called an
animational line of action.

00:03:20.810 --> 00:03:21.880
So he can change his posture.

00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:22.380
Right?

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:23.980
So he can look
like he's upright.

00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:27.460
Or he can stoop over or lean
to the side and cock his head.

00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:30.490
He can go through all of these
kind of expressive postures

00:03:30.490 --> 00:03:33.790
that are really foundational
to an animator when

00:03:33.790 --> 00:03:37.080
they want to express
something through characters.

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:37.820
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:42.430
Technologies tend to move
in rectilinear trajectories.

00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:44.660
Living things move in arcs.

00:03:44.660 --> 00:03:47.660
And so all of Jibo's
motions was based on arcs.

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:48.160
Right?

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:50.535
So he naturally moved in a
way that just seemed much more

00:03:50.535 --> 00:03:52.630
lifelike and expressive,
because he fundamentally

00:03:52.630 --> 00:03:54.940
was designed to move in arcs.

00:03:54.940 --> 00:03:56.357
You have to see Jibo move.

00:03:56.357 --> 00:03:57.940
You have to actually
watch them dance.

00:03:57.940 --> 00:04:01.420
Because it's incredible
to watch this robot dance.

00:04:01.420 --> 00:04:02.200
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:04.990
SARAH HANSEN: Jibo didn't
just look and move differently

00:04:04.990 --> 00:04:06.340
from other robots.

00:04:06.340 --> 00:04:09.840
He functioned differently too.

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:12.240
CYNTHIA BREAZEAL: I often
use the language of there's

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:14.070
the Digital Assistant.

00:04:14.070 --> 00:04:15.570
Like, this is what
like Google and--

00:04:15.570 --> 00:04:17.153
they're all called
digital assistants.

00:04:17.153 --> 00:04:19.800
And they're kind of modeled
after the executive assistant.

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:22.980
We talk about Jibo as
being a helpful companion.

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:25.192
So that speaks more to
the social, emotional,

00:04:25.192 --> 00:04:26.400
the relational aspects of it.

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:27.930
But Jibo is also helpful.

00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:29.730
He can actually do things.

00:04:29.730 --> 00:04:34.590
But it's the way he does things
are much more humanistic, I

00:04:34.590 --> 00:04:37.650
would say, human-centered in
its design, less transactional.

00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:41.220
The way he expresses
himself through movement

00:04:41.220 --> 00:04:45.730
and also just the language
he uses, it's much more warm,

00:04:45.730 --> 00:04:46.230
I would say.

00:04:46.230 --> 00:04:47.610
Like there's cool tech.

00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:49.230
Jibo is warm tech.

00:04:49.230 --> 00:04:50.310
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:04:50.310 --> 00:04:52.530
SARAH HANSEN: One exciting
new application for Jibo

00:04:52.530 --> 00:04:54.840
that's currently
being explored is

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:57.900
how he might help provide
mental health care.

00:04:57.900 --> 00:04:59.400
CYNTHIA BREAZEAL:
One of the studies

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:03.420
we did, we literally designed
an emotional wellness coach

00:05:03.420 --> 00:05:05.160
skill for Jibo.

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:08.010
And we deployed Jibo
in MIT student dorms.

00:05:08.010 --> 00:05:12.780
Because as you probably know,
mental health is a huge issue.

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:15.910
I mean, even before the
pandemic, mental health,

00:05:15.910 --> 00:05:19.080
emotional wellness of students
at a lot of top colleges

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:21.030
was really concerning.

00:05:21.030 --> 00:05:25.320
So we decided to explore whether
Jibo as an emotional wellness

00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:29.280
coach could actually help
mitigate the stress and anxiety

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:30.840
that students
experience when they're

00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:34.470
at an elite university like MIT.

00:05:34.470 --> 00:05:38.040
So we developed some
best-in-breed therapeutic

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:41.040
interactions that
typically a human would do,

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:45.150
like a coach would do with
their client or whatever.

00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:47.190
So typically it's human-human.

00:05:47.190 --> 00:05:50.130
We took these proven
methods and we just,

00:05:50.130 --> 00:05:53.460
now Jibo is just doing
it with the person.

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:54.840
And we published that work.

00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:56.257
And basically the
punch line is we

00:05:56.257 --> 00:05:58.200
were able to show
that, yeah, Jibo seemed

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:00.340
to make a positive difference.

00:06:00.340 --> 00:06:02.910
So in terms of the
future of health,

00:06:02.910 --> 00:06:04.680
people could go into
hospitals potentially

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:06.150
and get world-quality care.

00:06:06.150 --> 00:06:08.230
But what happens
when they go home?

00:06:08.230 --> 00:06:11.640
So again, something like
a Jibo, or a social robot,

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:15.228
or whatever, the idea that they
can extend the quality of care

00:06:15.228 --> 00:06:17.520
and support from the doctors
and nurses in the hospital

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:20.940
to the home where patients
are living every day, that's

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:22.368
a huge unmet need.

00:06:22.368 --> 00:06:23.910
And that's a role
where potentially I

00:06:23.910 --> 00:06:27.270
think an intelligent kind
of, again, collaborative

00:06:27.270 --> 00:06:30.840
allied agent with this different
kind of interaction paradigm

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:33.548
could make a big difference.

00:06:33.548 --> 00:06:35.340
SARAH HANSEN: One of
the fascinating design

00:06:35.340 --> 00:06:38.520
decisions behind Jibo
was choosing just

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:40.950
how human to make him.

00:06:40.950 --> 00:06:43.440
CYNTHIA BREAZEAL: When
we developed Jibo,

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:45.900
we were very
intentional in saying

00:06:45.900 --> 00:06:49.140
there's the character of Jibo.

00:06:49.140 --> 00:06:52.380
When you talk about
the perception of how

00:06:52.380 --> 00:06:55.020
children or adults
perceive these agents,

00:06:55.020 --> 00:06:59.640
Jibo as the character was very
aware that Jibo was a robot.

00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:02.582
And if you tried to ask
Jibo things that really

00:07:02.582 --> 00:07:04.290
a robot would have no
business answering,

00:07:04.290 --> 00:07:06.790
like things around religion or
whatnot, Jibo would say this,

00:07:06.790 --> 00:07:08.002
like hey, I'm just a robot.

00:07:08.002 --> 00:07:09.460
You know, that's
probably something

00:07:09.460 --> 00:07:11.502
that'd be good to talk
about another person with.

00:07:11.502 --> 00:07:12.720
But I'm just a robot.

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:15.870
So Jibo was very
upfront and transparent

00:07:15.870 --> 00:07:17.040
about his robot-ness.

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:18.900
[LAUGHS]

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:22.200
SARAH HANSEN: Collaborative
allied agents like Jibo

00:07:22.200 --> 00:07:25.830
have huge potential to
improve the lives of humans.

00:07:25.830 --> 00:07:29.400
But as Cynthia pointed out,
they can't replace the power

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:32.930
of real human social networks.

00:07:32.930 --> 00:07:34.790
CYNTHIA BREAZEAL: This
gets into the ethics

00:07:34.790 --> 00:07:36.582
and the responsible
design of these things.

00:07:36.582 --> 00:07:40.400
I think, first and foremost,
people need people.

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:44.000
We need our human social
networks, our in-person,

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:47.270
caring human social
networks to come around us

00:07:47.270 --> 00:07:49.018
when we're in trouble.

00:07:49.018 --> 00:07:50.810
Social media companies
will try to tell you

00:07:50.810 --> 00:07:52.602
your network of 5,000
people you don't know

00:07:52.602 --> 00:07:55.610
is just as important as
your actual, real, in-person

00:07:55.610 --> 00:07:57.200
relationships.

00:07:57.200 --> 00:08:00.140
Real, in-person relationships
are absolutely essential.

00:08:00.140 --> 00:08:02.165
They're essential
for our well-being.

00:08:02.165 --> 00:08:04.040
They're essential for
children's development.

00:08:04.040 --> 00:08:05.290
I mean, they're just critical.

00:08:05.290 --> 00:08:08.270
And so it signals, I think
we're living in this time

00:08:08.270 --> 00:08:12.530
when people's time and
attention has become

00:08:12.530 --> 00:08:14.540
this incredibly
precious resource where

00:08:14.540 --> 00:08:19.010
the fact that someone will stop
and take that time out for you

00:08:19.010 --> 00:08:21.860
signals something
of you're valued.

00:08:21.860 --> 00:08:24.050
You are really valued,
and you're meaningful.

00:08:24.050 --> 00:08:25.348
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:08:26.710 --> 00:08:28.450
SARAH HANSEN: As a
parent, the importance

00:08:28.450 --> 00:08:30.790
of real social
networks for children

00:08:30.790 --> 00:08:33.070
really feels true to me.

00:08:33.070 --> 00:08:35.890
But I also know that my child
is growing up in a world

00:08:35.890 --> 00:08:38.409
where digital
interactions are becoming

00:08:38.409 --> 00:08:41.530
more and more common
and potentially fraught

00:08:41.530 --> 00:08:43.909
with ethical implications.

00:08:43.909 --> 00:08:45.910
So I shared with
Cynthia a recording

00:08:45.910 --> 00:08:51.180
of my child talking with our
home executive assistant.

00:08:51.180 --> 00:08:54.120
CHILD: Alexa, what's
your favorite color?

00:08:54.120 --> 00:08:55.890
AMAZON ALEXA: I
like ultraviolent.

00:08:55.890 --> 00:08:57.790
It glows with everything.

00:08:57.790 --> 00:09:00.180
And here's another
fun fact about me.

00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:02.310
If you'd like, I
can learn your voice

00:09:02.310 --> 00:09:05.970
to do all sorts of neat things
like greeting you by name.

00:09:05.970 --> 00:09:08.040
You'll need your own profile.

00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:10.170
I can create one for you now.

00:09:10.170 --> 00:09:12.400
What's your name?

00:09:12.400 --> 00:09:14.280
CHILD: Alexa, off.

00:09:14.280 --> 00:09:16.290
AMAZON ALEXA: I'll
create a new profile

00:09:16.290 --> 00:09:18.540
and send information
about Alexa terms

00:09:18.540 --> 00:09:20.940
and our privacy
notice to your app.

00:09:20.940 --> 00:09:23.820
Should I go ahead and create
a profile with the name "Off?"

00:09:23.820 --> 00:09:26.122
Parent: "Alexa, off."

00:09:26.122 --> 00:09:27.080
CYNTHIA BREAZEAL: Yeah.

00:09:27.080 --> 00:09:34.580
So as illustrated by that clip,
kids are growing up in a time

00:09:34.580 --> 00:09:37.610
when they will have always
been able to talk and interact

00:09:37.610 --> 00:09:41.450
with intelligent machines, and
not just in this verbal way

00:09:41.450 --> 00:09:43.980
that we heard in the
clip, but hidden ways.

00:09:43.980 --> 00:09:44.480
Right?

00:09:44.480 --> 00:09:47.390
So when you're on
social media and it's

00:09:47.390 --> 00:09:51.890
trying to learn kind of
what your preferences are,

00:09:51.890 --> 00:09:54.470
and giving you
recommendations that

00:09:54.470 --> 00:09:57.470
are not trying to broaden
your perspective but

00:09:57.470 --> 00:10:00.110
if anything narrow, and
narrow, and narrow, and narrow.

00:10:00.110 --> 00:10:03.800
Or of course what we're learning
is, what gets your attention?

00:10:03.800 --> 00:10:05.630
What tends to get shared?

00:10:05.630 --> 00:10:07.480
Things that really upset you.

00:10:07.480 --> 00:10:10.055
[LAUGHS] Those are the things
that really-- you know?

00:10:10.055 --> 00:10:12.560
So that's what it
tends to feed you

00:10:12.560 --> 00:10:14.900
surprisingly quickly, right?

00:10:14.900 --> 00:10:17.120
So it's important for
people of all ages,

00:10:17.120 --> 00:10:18.620
but I think it's
important for kids.

00:10:18.620 --> 00:10:21.230
Because the average
age of when kids

00:10:21.230 --> 00:10:24.260
get their first smartphone,
it's like 10 years old.

00:10:24.260 --> 00:10:28.490
And social interaction
through technology

00:10:28.490 --> 00:10:34.680
becomes such a key way they
interact with each other.

00:10:34.680 --> 00:10:37.190
So the digital
mediated communication

00:10:37.190 --> 00:10:39.740
is, it just becomes such
an important way for them

00:10:39.740 --> 00:10:42.560
to connect and
express themselves.

00:10:42.560 --> 00:10:47.990
And just understanding what's
under the hood, how it could

00:10:47.990 --> 00:10:54.350
be biasing, shaping their
attitudes, potentially shaping

00:10:54.350 --> 00:10:55.440
behaviors.

00:10:55.440 --> 00:10:57.170
This is what's really critical--

00:10:57.170 --> 00:10:58.130
and why.

00:10:58.130 --> 00:11:01.630
Who's doing it and why?

00:11:01.630 --> 00:11:03.580
SARAH HANSEN: That's
where RAISE comes in.

00:11:03.580 --> 00:11:07.480
RAISE, which stands for
Responsible AI for Social

00:11:07.480 --> 00:11:11.020
Empowerment and Education,
is an MIT initiative

00:11:11.020 --> 00:11:14.950
focused on exactly these
kinds of questions.

00:11:14.950 --> 00:11:17.280
CYNTHIA BREAZEAL: It's kind
of the cross section of AI

00:11:17.280 --> 00:11:19.960
and learning, AI and education.

00:11:19.960 --> 00:11:21.630
So how can we develop
AI systems that

00:11:21.630 --> 00:11:26.250
help us learn, as well as
how can we understand how

00:11:26.250 --> 00:11:31.020
to best empower people to
learn about AI so that they

00:11:31.020 --> 00:11:35.430
can be informed citizens in how
they use these technologies?

00:11:35.430 --> 00:11:37.920
And how do we cultivate
the future designers

00:11:37.920 --> 00:11:41.460
of these technologies that
are responsible and ethical

00:11:41.460 --> 00:11:46.110
in their designs, but also a
far more diverse and inclusive

00:11:46.110 --> 00:11:47.250
future workforce?

00:11:47.250 --> 00:11:50.100
We love to position the
student as the designer

00:11:50.100 --> 00:11:53.160
of these technologies, so
as the active person who's

00:11:53.160 --> 00:11:56.050
thinking about the design, the
social implications, the goals,

00:11:56.050 --> 00:11:57.210
all of that.

00:11:57.210 --> 00:11:59.640
It really empowers
students in a way

00:11:59.640 --> 00:12:03.690
that they lean in, and discuss
and are invested in it.

00:12:03.690 --> 00:12:06.270
I think you want
children to grow up,

00:12:06.270 --> 00:12:08.250
students to grow up
with the attitude

00:12:08.250 --> 00:12:10.530
that I actually can
shape these things.

00:12:10.530 --> 00:12:14.970
This is a world that I can be
in, and belong in, and shape.

00:12:14.970 --> 00:12:17.100
We have both a research
kind of objective.

00:12:17.100 --> 00:12:20.940
We have an outreach,
k-12 outreach, objective.

00:12:20.940 --> 00:12:23.790
And a lot of that is
bringing these materials

00:12:23.790 --> 00:12:26.430
to teachers and students
of underrepresented,

00:12:26.430 --> 00:12:28.350
under-resourced schools
and communities.

00:12:28.350 --> 00:12:31.830
Because the field is not
diverse or inclusive.

00:12:31.830 --> 00:12:34.350
We need to take action
to correct that.

00:12:34.350 --> 00:12:37.440
And so we have an explicit
diversity and inclusion

00:12:37.440 --> 00:12:40.380
mission in what we're
doing through the outreach.

00:12:40.380 --> 00:12:45.120
And we are trying to design
the curriculum, the concepts,

00:12:45.120 --> 00:12:47.850
the activities to
meet students where

00:12:47.850 --> 00:12:50.970
they are in things that they
use and they think about so it's

00:12:50.970 --> 00:12:53.340
super relevant to them now.

00:12:53.340 --> 00:12:57.450
But then we also expose them
to the way AI is transforming

00:12:57.450 --> 00:13:00.720
so many disciplines, and
markets, and industries

00:13:00.720 --> 00:13:02.700
so they understand
it's here and it's

00:13:02.700 --> 00:13:05.863
going to continue to
shape our futures.

00:13:05.863 --> 00:13:07.530
SARAH HANSEN: Part
of that work involves

00:13:07.530 --> 00:13:11.040
offering an opportunity for
young people and their teachers

00:13:11.040 --> 00:13:13.710
across the country to
participate in the upcoming

00:13:13.710 --> 00:13:18.237
Day of AI on May 13th, 2022.

00:13:18.237 --> 00:13:19.820
CYNTHIA BREAZEAL:
We develop all kinds

00:13:19.820 --> 00:13:24.380
of curriculum of different
formats and different lengths.

00:13:24.380 --> 00:13:28.580
So Day of AI is the
introductory experience.

00:13:28.580 --> 00:13:32.720
And it's really an opportunity,
if teachers or students

00:13:32.720 --> 00:13:37.040
are just curious about AI,
it is literally a program

00:13:37.040 --> 00:13:40.610
intended to be for
any kind of teacher,

00:13:40.610 --> 00:13:43.910
for students from elementary
up to high school,

00:13:43.910 --> 00:13:49.100
where they can learn
about AI from more

00:13:49.100 --> 00:13:52.130
of the lens of its implications
on digital literacy

00:13:52.130 --> 00:13:55.770
and digital citizenship, but
also demystifying it as well.

00:13:55.770 --> 00:13:58.580
So all great bands
kind of have this,

00:13:58.580 --> 00:14:00.870
what is AI introductory module?

00:14:00.870 --> 00:14:03.590
So for instance, like
when I spoke about this AI

00:14:03.590 --> 00:14:07.040
and creativity curriculum, we've
had students who are saying,

00:14:07.040 --> 00:14:09.890
I didn't think I was
relevant or important to me

00:14:09.890 --> 00:14:12.470
because I want to be a
photographer when I grow up.

00:14:12.470 --> 00:14:15.020
But they're like,
now I realize, wow,

00:14:15.020 --> 00:14:16.970
AI could actually be a
really cool tool that I

00:14:16.970 --> 00:14:19.765
can use creatively in my
profession of photography

00:14:19.765 --> 00:14:20.390
when I grow up.

00:14:20.390 --> 00:14:24.290
So a lot of this just to help
kids to understand that chances

00:14:24.290 --> 00:14:27.020
are, this is something
that's going to be

00:14:27.020 --> 00:14:29.130
empowering and relevant to you.

00:14:29.130 --> 00:14:32.450
So just be aware of it,
being able to leverage

00:14:32.450 --> 00:14:34.760
it to help you
achieve your goals

00:14:34.760 --> 00:14:36.338
and your ambitions in life.

00:14:36.338 --> 00:14:37.130
SARAH HANSEN: Well.

00:14:37.130 --> 00:14:39.200
If there's a teacher
out there like me

00:14:39.200 --> 00:14:42.650
who sometimes can't even get
her agent to turn the lights on,

00:14:42.650 --> 00:14:44.202
is this still appropriate?

00:14:44.202 --> 00:14:45.410
CYNTHIA BREAZEAL: Absolutely.

00:14:45.410 --> 00:14:47.400
So it's for all
kinds of teachers.

00:14:47.400 --> 00:14:47.900
Right?

00:14:47.900 --> 00:14:51.260
This is not a technical,
computer science,

00:14:51.260 --> 00:14:53.000
STEM curriculum at all.

00:14:53.000 --> 00:14:55.010
It's really much
more around, again,

00:14:55.010 --> 00:14:59.090
digital citizenship and digital
literacy as it pertains to AI,

00:14:59.090 --> 00:15:02.180
so again, AI in our lives,
and understanding that,

00:15:02.180 --> 00:15:05.030
and having conversations
about that,

00:15:05.030 --> 00:15:07.260
and designing some cool
things around that.

00:15:07.260 --> 00:15:07.760
[LAUGHS]

00:15:07.760 --> 00:15:08.660
SARAH HANSEN: Yeah.

00:15:08.660 --> 00:15:11.660
So where can teachers
go to sign up?

00:15:11.660 --> 00:15:13.968
And is there a fee associated?

00:15:13.968 --> 00:15:15.260
CYNTHIA BREAZEAL: So it's free.

00:15:15.260 --> 00:15:17.150
Again, one of the
great things about MIT

00:15:17.150 --> 00:15:20.940
is we want to lower the barrier
as much as humanly possible.

00:15:20.940 --> 00:15:22.620
So all the materials are free.

00:15:22.620 --> 00:15:25.280
You can go to DayofAI.org.

00:15:25.280 --> 00:15:28.500
You can look at the
curriculum right there.

00:15:28.500 --> 00:15:31.100
We also offer free
teacher training.

00:15:31.100 --> 00:15:32.990
You can register
and you can sign up

00:15:32.990 --> 00:15:34.940
for an online training session.

00:15:34.940 --> 00:15:37.880
We are collaborating
with I2 Learning.

00:15:37.880 --> 00:15:40.280
This is a company that what
they do is train teachers.

00:15:40.280 --> 00:15:41.280
This is what they do.

00:15:41.280 --> 00:15:45.860
And so we've worked with them to
take our curriculum materials,

00:15:45.860 --> 00:15:48.980
and using kind of their
experience to really tune it

00:15:48.980 --> 00:15:51.030
for what they think
is great for, again,

00:15:51.030 --> 00:15:53.300
a broad, general
teacher audience.

00:15:53.300 --> 00:15:56.330
And they are running the
teacher training sessions.

00:15:56.330 --> 00:15:58.470
And again, it's completely free.

00:15:58.470 --> 00:16:01.830
So we would love for
teachers to sign up.

00:16:01.830 --> 00:16:04.400
We are in almost
all 50 states now.

00:16:04.400 --> 00:16:05.630
[LAUGHS]

00:16:05.630 --> 00:16:07.460
SARAH HANSEN: RAISE
and the Day of AI

00:16:07.460 --> 00:16:10.790
are increasing access
to AI education.

00:16:10.790 --> 00:16:14.150
And they're also
informing how we teach it.

00:16:14.150 --> 00:16:16.190
CYNTHIA BREAZEAL: It
is an open question

00:16:16.190 --> 00:16:21.080
still about what are the most
effective ways of educating

00:16:21.080 --> 00:16:24.080
students at a variety
of ages and backgrounds

00:16:24.080 --> 00:16:26.360
about what artificial
intelligence is

00:16:26.360 --> 00:16:27.800
and how it works.

00:16:27.800 --> 00:16:29.510
We still have much
we need to understand

00:16:29.510 --> 00:16:32.000
in terms of what grades,
what preparation,

00:16:32.000 --> 00:16:35.772
what concepts, how
can we demystify

00:16:35.772 --> 00:16:37.730
and these computational
methods in a way that's

00:16:37.730 --> 00:16:39.380
grade and age-appropriate?

00:16:39.380 --> 00:16:41.240
How we can prepare
teachers-- there's

00:16:41.240 --> 00:16:42.990
a whole bunch of
research questions there.

00:16:42.990 --> 00:16:44.810
What are the right
approaches, methods

00:16:44.810 --> 00:16:47.455
of professional development
for teachers who, I mean,

00:16:47.455 --> 00:16:48.080
I can tell you.

00:16:48.080 --> 00:16:51.980
They didn't major in
AI in college, right?

00:16:51.980 --> 00:16:54.080
So this is new to them too.

00:16:54.080 --> 00:16:56.360
So how do we do
that effectively?

00:16:56.360 --> 00:16:58.610
What are the longer
impacts of this

00:16:58.610 --> 00:17:01.700
in terms of attitudes
and dispositions

00:17:01.700 --> 00:17:04.010
that students may form
about these technologies,

00:17:04.010 --> 00:17:06.710
and about themselves,
and their relationship

00:17:06.710 --> 00:17:08.569
to these technologies?

00:17:08.569 --> 00:17:12.470
And then as we do this, how do
we again holistically develop

00:17:12.470 --> 00:17:13.710
the whole person as well?

00:17:13.710 --> 00:17:16.349
So teamwork, collaboration,
communication,

00:17:16.349 --> 00:17:19.190
critical thinking, creative--

00:17:19.190 --> 00:17:22.760
they can all be kind of woven
into this tapestry of how

00:17:22.760 --> 00:17:27.470
we try to address AI
literacy through positioning

00:17:27.470 --> 00:17:29.450
students as the
designers, and creators,

00:17:29.450 --> 00:17:32.580
and makers of these solutions
with these technologies.

00:17:32.580 --> 00:17:36.230
So we design these activities.

00:17:36.230 --> 00:17:38.010
And then we evaluate them.

00:17:38.010 --> 00:17:38.510
Right?

00:17:38.510 --> 00:17:42.810
So we will reach out and
recruit students and teachers.

00:17:42.810 --> 00:17:44.300
And we will engage them.

00:17:44.300 --> 00:17:46.440
We often engage in
co-design with them.

00:17:46.440 --> 00:17:48.410
So even as we're
developing these materials,

00:17:48.410 --> 00:17:50.390
we will work with
teachers and students

00:17:50.390 --> 00:17:54.950
as we develop them to make
sure that they're effective,

00:17:54.950 --> 00:17:56.690
and responding, and engaging.

00:17:56.690 --> 00:17:59.840
And then we do
rigorous evaluations

00:17:59.840 --> 00:18:02.990
of what are they understanding
or not understanding?

00:18:02.990 --> 00:18:04.922
What is their pathway
to understanding?

00:18:04.922 --> 00:18:06.380
And what is the
implication of that

00:18:06.380 --> 00:18:09.380
for how we think about staging
these concepts and ideas?

00:18:09.380 --> 00:18:13.557
Are we seeing evidence that
they are developing dispositions

00:18:13.557 --> 00:18:15.140
about AI that are
empowering for them,

00:18:15.140 --> 00:18:17.800
or do they find it as being
intimidating or not for them?

00:18:17.800 --> 00:18:21.100
So there's a lot of questions
that we're trying to assess.

00:18:21.100 --> 00:18:24.430
And then when we have
something we think is solid,

00:18:24.430 --> 00:18:26.990
then we make it
available for free.

00:18:26.990 --> 00:18:28.360
It's all Creative Commons.

00:18:28.360 --> 00:18:34.030
Our goal is just to help
empower teachers, non-profits,

00:18:34.030 --> 00:18:37.210
get these materials out
there and share them freely.

00:18:37.210 --> 00:18:38.890
SARAH HANSEN: But
again, no matter how

00:18:38.890 --> 00:18:42.040
good robots or AI get,
they're no replacement

00:18:42.040 --> 00:18:44.020
for the real thing.

00:18:44.020 --> 00:18:45.850
CYNTHIA BREAZEAL:
I think a lot of AI

00:18:45.850 --> 00:18:47.762
has assumed when
you're trying to build

00:18:47.762 --> 00:18:49.970
the social and emotional
qualities into technologies,

00:18:49.970 --> 00:18:51.910
it's about the robot.

00:18:51.910 --> 00:18:54.310
And I'm like, no,
it's about the people

00:18:54.310 --> 00:18:57.670
because this is what
people need to engage.

00:18:57.670 --> 00:19:01.510
The more deeply we can
engage of our holistic self,

00:19:01.510 --> 00:19:04.203
not just our cognitive
selves, but our social selves,

00:19:04.203 --> 00:19:06.370
our emotional selves, our
physical selves-- the more

00:19:06.370 --> 00:19:09.100
deeply we can engage,
not surprisingly,

00:19:09.100 --> 00:19:11.720
the more successful we are,
the better our outcomes.

00:19:11.720 --> 00:19:13.810
So it's to say,
it's not about let's

00:19:13.810 --> 00:19:16.810
build machines that look like
people and act like people.

00:19:16.810 --> 00:19:20.110
It's, let's build machines
that dovetail with us in a way

00:19:20.110 --> 00:19:22.900
that we can bring
forth all of ourselves

00:19:22.900 --> 00:19:24.670
to help us achieve
the goals that

00:19:24.670 --> 00:19:26.360
are really important to us.

00:19:26.360 --> 00:19:30.518
And this is why when we talk
about AI as pedagogical agents,

00:19:30.518 --> 00:19:32.560
I'm like, we will never
get rid of human teachers

00:19:32.560 --> 00:19:35.920
because they are
essential for children

00:19:35.920 --> 00:19:40.330
to know that in this
community of human beings,

00:19:40.330 --> 00:19:45.250
adults invest their time
in children's learning

00:19:45.250 --> 00:19:46.090
and development.

00:19:46.090 --> 00:19:47.410
Like, you matter.

00:19:47.410 --> 00:19:49.150
I'm taking time and
energy to do this

00:19:49.150 --> 00:19:50.317
because it's that important.

00:19:50.317 --> 00:19:51.500
And you are that important.

00:19:51.500 --> 00:19:54.054
You know, you just can't
underestimate that.

00:19:54.054 --> 00:19:56.710
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:19:56.710 --> 00:19:59.830
SARAH HANSEN: To take part
in the Day of AI on May 13,

00:19:59.830 --> 00:20:02.380
go to DayofAI.org.

00:20:02.380 --> 00:20:05.440
You can access free teacher
training and other Creative

00:20:05.440 --> 00:20:08.380
Commons educational
materials at RAISE.MIT.edu.

00:20:08.380 --> 00:20:14.128
Or you can access them through
our website at OCW.MIT.edu.

00:20:16.930 --> 00:20:19.690
You can also find Cynthia's
free and open teaching materials

00:20:19.690 --> 00:20:22.820
from her MIT courses
on our website.

00:20:22.820 --> 00:20:25.010
Thank you so much for listening.

00:20:25.010 --> 00:20:29.380
Until next time, signing off
from a galaxy not too far away,

00:20:29.380 --> 00:20:33.490
I'm Sarah Hansen from
MIT OpenCourseWare.

